## Boot Compute Nodes

Boot the compute nodes; they load the packed image over the network into RAM.
Because the resource manager was started above, each node's scheduler daemon
finds the head node as soon as it comes up. After the initial PXE boot, subsequent
boots return to the default BIOS boot device.

{% include "provisioner/xcat/power-cycle.md.j2" %}

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Wait until every compute node has netbooted and sshd responds. -->
<!-- ohpc_command # A fixed sleep races a slow netboot; poll node state instead and -->
<!-- ohpc_command # bound the wait so a failed boot cannot hang the recipe forever. -->
<!-- ohpc_command boot_timeout=${boot_timeout:-900} -->
<!-- ohpc_command deadline=$(( SECONDS + boot_timeout )) -->
<!-- ohpc_command while true; do -->
<!-- ohpc_command     booted=$(nodestat compute | grep -c ": sshd" || true) -->
<!-- ohpc_command     [ "$booted" -eq "$num_computes" ] && break -->
<!-- ohpc_command     if [ "$SECONDS" -ge "$deadline" ]; then -->
<!-- ohpc_command         echo "ERROR: after ${boot_timeout}s, $booted of $num_computes nodes reported sshd." >&2 -->
<!-- ohpc_command         echo "Inspect with: nodestat compute; rcons <node>" >&2 -->
<!-- ohpc_command         exit 1 -->
<!-- ohpc_command     fi -->
<!-- ohpc_command     echo "$booted of $num_computes compute nodes ready" -->
<!-- ohpc_command     sleep 10 -->
<!-- ohpc_command done -->
<!-- ohpc_end -->

The boot process typically completes in about 5 minutes. Monitor provisioning
with the `rcons` command, which displays the serial console for a selected node.
The escape sequence to exit `rcons` is `Ctrl-e c .` typed sequentially.

Once booted, verify successful provisioning with a parallel command across the
compute nodes. xCAT provides `xdsh`, its native distributed shell, which
accepts xCAT node names and groups:

```bash
xdsh compute uptime
c1:  12:56:50 up 14 min,  0 users,  load average: 0.00, 0.01, 0.04
c2:  12:56:50 up 13 min,  0 users,  load average: 0.00, 0.02, 0.05
c3:  12:56:50 up 14 min,  0 users,  load average: 0.00, 0.02, 0.05
c4:  12:56:50 up 14 min,  0 users,  load average: 0.00, 0.01, 0.04
```

The provisioner-independent `pdsh` utility, installed on the head node as part
of the `ohpc-base` metapackage, may be used as well:

```bash
pdsh -w ${compute_prefix}[1-${num_computes}] uptime
```
